{"authored" [{"question" "q-107999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $2\\,m$ when its velocity is $8\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-107999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/017463f3ac8abac9c2b00c4d9a715a4109a5f4bc1b070e0a12c4d3a7bac64351.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=2\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=8\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =2(2)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =32-8 \\\\ C & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+24 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}+48 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}+12) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-107999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-107999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/3c5450b18c5dc58b082faadf4086b6a21c0c9b55afb016090147609a2ccfe719.png)\n\nSince the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int [-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={3}^{1-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\sqrt{2})^{2} & ={3}^{1-2}+C \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-1}+\\frac{11}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{28}{3}} \\\\  & ≈-3.1\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1.8\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2.3\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2.9\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3.7\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ its acceleration is $2(x+2)^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t$ where $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{4}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/ba9988a12d1c70c43a6a010c3578850fe79a9bca01309884fe981b3f7cca512c.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2(x+2)^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2(x+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2(x+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2(x+2)^{3}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+2)^{4}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =(x+2)^{4} \\\\ v & =\\pm (x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(x+2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x+2} & =t+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+2} & =-t-C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x+2}=-t+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+2} & =-t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+2} & =\\frac{1-2t}{2} \\\\ x+2 & =\\frac{2}{1-2t} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{1-2t}-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4t}{1-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "030db5ad2fbf35e21a7d16912e477215bf6633d681e93bacafc91906df729431" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=2t-{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{4t}{1-2t}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{t}{1-t}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-2t}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=t-4{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{4t}{1-2t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5dae5fb2bbcaa6bfc15e487b5228cb33fee04bf434ad1d7bbcb9a7500e9ba5c" "basis" 1531 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-107999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $2\\,m$ when its velocity is $8\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-107999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/assets/017463f3ac8abac9c2b00c4d9a715a4109a5f4bc1b070e0a12c4d3a7bac64351.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=2\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=8\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =2(2)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =32-8 \\\\ C & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+24 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}+48 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}+12) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-107999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "be38e6ea80fd51fa9e27403513647ec9533fa23df5c790c8fd8c39368a5c7aca"} {"question" "q-107999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-107999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}"] ["math" "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}"] ["math" "v(x)=2{x}^{2}"] ["math" "v(x)=\\frac{8}{5}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+21}"] ["math" "v(x)={x}^{2}+4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-107999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-108004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $2{e}^{-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=2\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-108004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/assets/ad437638f22672a10a183f1521929637f8d43725f37a9736c569516232e7c468.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $2{e}^{-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-2{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-2{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -2{e}^{-x}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{-x}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=0\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{-x}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =2{e}^{0}+K \\\\ K & =2-2 \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{-x} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ v & =\\pm 2{e}^{-x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2{e}^{-x/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2{e}^{-x/2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2{e}^{-x/2} \\\\ {e}^{x/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2 \\\\ \\int {e}^{x/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{x/2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ 2{e}^{x/2} & =2t+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{x/2} & =t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{e}^{x/2}=t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x/2} & =t+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x/2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1) \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-108004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "be38e6ea80fd51fa9e27403513647ec9533fa23df5c790c8fd8c39368a5c7aca"} {"question" "q-108004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-108004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=2({e}^{t}-1)"] ["math" "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)"] ["math" "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)"] ["math" "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)"] ["math" "x(t)={e}^{2t}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-108004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-122314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=3x\\,m/s.$ If $x=2\\,m$ when $t=0,$ find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-122314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/assets/efdb69673b4088d3c021d78282642ff7abc57158e23704cd3d5e7ff2559cbdbb.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=3x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x| & =t+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|x| & =3t+3K \\\\ x & ={e}^{3t+3K} \\\\ x & =C{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=2$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=2,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=2{e}^{3t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-122314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "be38e6ea80fd51fa9e27403513647ec9533fa23df5c790c8fd8c39368a5c7aca"} {"question" "q-122314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-122314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=2{e}^{3t}"] ["math" "x(t)=2{e}^{3{t}^{2}}"] ["math" "x(t)=3{e}^{2t}"] ["math" "x(t)=3{e}^{2t}-1"] ["math" "x(t)={e}^{3t}+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-122314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=2{e}^{3t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ its acceleration is $2(x+2)^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t$ where $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{4}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/assets/ba9988a12d1c70c43a6a010c3578850fe79a9bca01309884fe981b3f7cca512c.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2(x+2)^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2(x+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2(x+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2(x+2)^{3}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+2)^{4}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =(x+2)^{4} \\\\ v & =\\pm (x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(x+2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x+2} & =t+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+2} & =-t-C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x+2}=-t+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+2} & =-t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+2} & =\\frac{1-2t}{2} \\\\ x+2 & =\\frac{2}{1-2t} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{1-2t}-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4t}{1-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "be38e6ea80fd51fa9e27403513647ec9533fa23df5c790c8fd8c39368a5c7aca"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=2t-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{4t}{1-2t}"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{t}{1-t}"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-2t}"] ["math" "x(t)=t-4{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-122344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{4t}{1-2t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/assets/3c5450b18c5dc58b082faadf4086b6a21c0c9b55afb016090147609a2ccfe719.png)\n\nSince the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int [-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={3}^{1-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\sqrt{2})^{2} & ={3}^{1-2}+C \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-1}+\\frac{11}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{28}{3}} \\\\  & ≈-3.1\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "be38e6ea80fd51fa9e27403513647ec9533fa23df5c790c8fd8c39368a5c7aca"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2a2907bbd8f40924d938a8cfe65e2711605a596c00b550594eb598648335b9e"} {"question" "q-122347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1.8\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "-2.3\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "-2.9\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "-3.7\\,\\text{m/s}"]] 